description:
pkix-lint/src/rfc5280.rs:95 (with_max_octets) and :151 (set_parameter) — Two API paths to the same parameter have opposite validation:

  pub fn with_max_octets(max_octets: usize) -> Self {
      ...
      Self { max_octets, parameters }  // accepts 0
  }

  fn set_parameter(&mut self, id: &str, value: &str) -> Result<(), ParameterError> {
      ...
      if parsed == 0 {
          return Err(ParameterError::InvalidValue { id, reason: "..." });
      }
      self.max_octets = parsed;
      Ok(())
  }

CALLER IMPACT: A caller writes either:
  // Path A — typed constructor
  let lint = Rfc5280MaxSerialLengthLint::with_max_octets(0);

  // Path B — string parameter
  let mut lint = Rfc5280MaxSerialLengthLint::default();
  lint.set_parameter("max-octets", "0")?;

Path A silently constructs a lint with max_octets=0. Every certificate has a serial number of at least 1 octet (RFC 5280 §4.1.2.2 requires positive non-zero), so this lint rejects EVERY cert with LintResult::Error. The caller sees finding output but cannot debug the configuration error from the symptom.

Path B returns ParameterError::InvalidValue with a clear "max-octets must be at least 1" message. The caller fixes their input.

The asymmetry produces a debugging trap: callers using the typed constructor get a silent misconfiguration; callers using set_parameter get diagnostic feedback.

This is a parameter-validation gap, not a documentation gap. PKIX-7f92.5 separately flags the OTHER direction (>20 silently weakens the RFC baseline). Both flaws apply to with_max_octets.

REMEDIATION OPTIONS:

1. Change with_max_octets parameter to NonZeroUsize:
   pub fn with_max_octets(max_octets: NonZeroUsize) -> Self
   Type-system enforcement; cleanest. Callers write NonZeroUsize::new(20).unwrap() or NonZeroUsize::new(20).expect("non-zero serial cap").

2. Make with_max_octets fallible:
   pub fn with_max_octets(max_octets: usize) -> Result<Self, ParameterError>
   Symmetrical with set_parameter; same error type. Less ergonomic for callers building config tables.

3. Panic in with_max_octets on 0:
   pub fn with_max_octets(max_octets: usize) -> Self {
       assert!(max_octets >= 1, "with_max_octets: max-octets must be at least 1");
       ...
   }
   Document # Panics. Matches LintRunner::new's panic-on-duplicate-id pattern (precedent in the crate).

Option 1 is most defensive and type-system-enforced; Option 3 is the minimum-invasive precedent-following choice.

Pre-1.0 free for any choice. PKIX-7f92.5 will need a similar treatment for the upper bound (20). Consider whether to combine both validations into a single typed range (e.g., wrap the parameter in a SerialOctetLimit newtype).
